<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Carmarthen</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Moridunum</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Carmarthen is the county town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carmarthenshire">Carmarthenshire</a> in south-west <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>, on the River Tywi. It began as Moridunum, the Roman town of the Demetae, which had an amphitheatre and is among the westernmost Roman towns in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>. In Welsh legend it was linked with the wizard Merlin, and the Black Book of Carmarthen, one of the oldest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> in Welsh, is named after its priory.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/carmarthen.jpg" alt="Carmarthen" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: M J Richardson · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Carmarthen_Guildhall_(geograph_5737601).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
